<Base
  title="Living in Andorra practitioner map | Official text still wins"
  description="Jane Whittaker’s 2026 livinginandorra.com site as an English practitioner map, read against Govern.ad, BOPA and AFA. Attributed where only she states it."
  path="/practitioner-map"
>
  <section class="page-header">
    <h1>An English practitioner map</h1>
    <p>
      Rob locked the whole Living in Andorra site — not only the category
      page — as a standing English resource. What follows is a reading of
      her current index against official text. We do not copy her prose.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <div class="notice">
      <p>
        Practitioner:
        <a
          href="https://www.livinginandorra.com/index.html"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener">livinginandorra.com</a
        >
        (Jane Whittaker, J W Serveis; index dated 19 August 2026). Official
        sources win. If a figure or operational rule appears only on her
        pages, we write “Living in Andorra states …” and link, or we omit
        it. Directory listing:
        <a href="/directory/jane-whittaker">Jane Whittaker — J W Serveis</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Two alphabets</h2>
    <p>
      Her Categories A–D are not the Government’s A.1, B.1, C.1 or D.1.
      The mapping is on
      <a href="/become-a-resident">become a resident</a>:
      her A → official D.1 without lucrative activity; her B → D.1.2; her
      C → D.1.3; her D (Compte Propi) → J.1. Official A.1 is employed
      residence and work. Official B.1 is
      <a href="/family-reunification">family reunification</a>.
      She states that she does not process digital-nomad or entrepreneur
      files; Govern.ad still publishes
      <a href="/digital-nomad">D.3</a>
      and
      <a href="/entrepreneur-residence">D.4</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Compte Propi / Category D — closed?</h2>
    <p>
      Living in Andorra states that Category D remains closed to new
      applications, that she has no reopening date, and that she
      speculates a new quota might slip into 2027 because of late-2026
      parliamentary elections and immigration-law work tied to the
      Schengen-related accord. She labels that last sentence speculation.
      Official publications still show a live
      <a
        href="https://www.govern.ad/ca/tematiques/immigracio/residencia-i-treball/residencia-i-treball-per-compte-propi-autoritzacio-inicial"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener">J.1 page</a
      >, a
      <a
        href="https://www.govern.ad/ca/tematiques/immigracio/residencia-i-treball/reserva-autoritzacio-de-residencia-i-treball-per-compte-propi"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener">J reservation</a
      >, and a
      <a
        href="https://www.govern.ad/ca/w/aprovada-una-nova-quota-d-autoritzacions-d-immigracio-de-residencia-i-treball-per-compte-propi-i-de-treball-sense-residencia"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener">Govern news note</a
      >
      on Decret 75/2026 (a general self-employed quota of 200). Confirm
      whether a place is actually being issued. Longer note:
      <a href="/self-employed-residence">J.1</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      On renewals, Living in Andorra states there is no legal minimum
      turnover, but that Immigration wants an actively trading company —
      “no invoicing, no activity” in her words. The official renewal pack
      asks for documents proving the company’s commercial activity in the
      period being renewed. It does not set a turnover floor. Do not treat
      her golden rule as law.
    </p>

    <h2>Passive investment — Law 2/2026</h2>
    <p>
      Her 13 February 2026 effective date and the €1 million / €800,000
      per unit / €400,000 Housing Fund figures match article 96 of
      <a
        href="https://bopadocuments.blob.core.windows.net/bopa-documents/038015/pdf/CGL_2026_02_12_10_11_12.pdf"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener">Law 2/2026 in the BOPA</a
      >. So do the €50,000 + €12,000 AFA incomes for residence without
      lucrative activity (definitive unless the initial authorisation is
      refused). Card-issue amounts of €3,000 / €1,000 and a €500
      residence-without-work renewal sit on the
      <a
        href="https://www.govern.ad/ca/tematiques/immigracio/altres-tramits/tarifes"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener">official tariffs page</a
      >. The 6% / 10% foreign-investment property tax is on a
      <a
        href="https://www.govern.ad/ca/w/s-actualitza-el-reglament-de-l-impost-sobre-la-inversio-estrangera-immobiliaria-d-acord-amb-la-nova-llei-de-continuitat-i-consolidacio-de-les-mesures-per-al-creixement-sostenible"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener">Govern news note of 26 February 2026</a
      >. Ninety days versus 183 days is
      <a href="/tax-residence">immigration versus tax residence</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Living in Andorra states that Immigration has not confirmed the
      Housing Fund is operating, and that she would treat Categories B and
      C as non-refundable for planning even though she thinks those bonds
      may still be refundable. Those two sentences are hers. Article 96
      names the fund “in accordance with the applicable regulations”; we
      have not found a Govern.ad application counter for it.
    </p>

    <h2>Passive renewals — local bank certificate</h2>
    <p>
      Living in Andorra states that Immigration now wants a local bank
      certificate for the annual income / minimum-wage multiple, and will
      no longer accept a foreign bank letter or a foreign tax return. The
      live
      <a
        href="https://www.govern.ad/ca/tematiques/immigracio/residencia/residencia-sense-treball-autoritzacio-de-renovacio"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener">D.2 page</a
      >
      still says the 300% + 100% formula is evidenced, among other
      things, by a pension certificate, a tax return from the last country
      of residence, and bank certifications. The official list is
      enunciative. Her “local only” rule is therefore attributed, not
      restated as law. Read D.2 and
      <a
        href="https://www.livinginandorra.com/residency_renewals.html"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener">her renewals page</a
      >
      with counsel.
    </p>

    <h2>Selling property — plusvàlua, not her commentary</h2>
    <p>
      Living in Andorra states that capital gains on a property sale have
      become complex for passive residents. The official taxes are the
      <a
        href="https://www.govern.ad/ca/l/4194252"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener">impost sobre les plusvàlues</a
      >
      (Law 21/2006, consolidated) and, where it applies, IRPF on capital
      gains. The Government’s IRPF guide describes an exemption for the
      habitual-and-permanent home when the seller is Andorran or can show
      five years’ legal, permanent and continuous residence, and
      reinvests in another habitual home here. We do not reprint her
      commentary or any rate. Instruct tax counsel before a sale.
    </p>

    <h2>EES and the border-management agreement</h2>
    <p>
      A
      <a
        href="https://www.govern.ad/ca/w/condicions-de-circulacio-amb-el-nou-sistema-europeu-entry-exit-ees-"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener">Govern.ad note of 2 April 2026</a
      >
      states that the European Entry/Exit System, rolled out from October
      2025, is fully operational from <strong>10 April 2026</strong> (her
      index says 11 April). Andorran nationals are exempt from EES
      registration under Regulation (EU) 2017/2226; so are EU / Schengen
      nationals and legal residents of a member state. The
      Andorra–EU border-management agreement has been initialled
      (rubricat) but is still pending signature, ratification and
      application —
      <a
        href="https://www.govern.ad/ca/w/andorra-i-la-unio-europea-rubriquen-l-acord-de-gestio-de-fronteres"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener">Govern news</a
      >
      and
      <a
        href="https://www.andorraue.ad/ca/acords-amb-la-ue/acord-de-gestio-de-fronteres/"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener">AndorraUE</a
      >.
    </p>
    <p>
      Until that agreement enters into force, the same official note tells
      third-country nationals who hold an Andorran residence permit and
      who wish to travel to Schengen states other than Spain and France to
      check the destination or transit requirements in good time. We do
      not publish her border-queue steps. When the agreement applies,
      Govern.ad says those residents, after a security assessment, should
      be able to make short Schengen stays without a visa, ETIAS or EES
      registration. That is a future official promise, not a present
      practice.
    </p>

    <h2>Driving licences</h2>
    <p>
      Living in Andorra states that photographs are now taken in digital
      booths at the Government or a Comú, with a digital signature, and
      that residency cards may follow. The official
      <a
        href="https://www.e-tramits.ad/tramits/renovacio-del-permis-de-conduir/p/GV000303"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener">e-tramits renewal</a
      >
      and first-issue pages say the photograph and signature are taken at
      Government premises, by appointment. That is as far as we go. We do
      not write a how-to, list exchange countries from her page, or repeat
      her import or instructor prices.
    </p>

    <h2>Cost of living</h2>
    <p>
      She publishes itemised cost guides, including
      <a
        href="http://www.livinginandorra.com/files/COST_2025.pdf"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener">COST_2025.pdf</a
      >. Those are her estimates, not official prices and not a quote
      from this site.
    </p>

    <h2>Russians and Belarusians</h2>
    <p>
      Living in Andorra states that foreign-investment company files (her
      B and D) are not being processed for those nationalities, that her
      Category A may still proceed, and that banks are difficult. We have
      not found a matching Govern.ad circular. Confirm with counsel and
      the Register of Foreign Investments.
    </p>

